WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:31.009
737 MAX 8:
When the Boeing 737 Max 8 first entered service

00:00:31.009 --> 00:00:37.079
in May of 2017, it was touted as being the
new standard for narrow body aviation.

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:43.260
And while this was a bold claim, no one really
contested it except maybe airbus.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:50.420
After all, 737s had been reliably flying since
1967, so it’s not to surprising that the

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:56.399
newest version of the 737 would take the crown
of being the most reliable and the most efficient.

00:00:56.399 --> 00:01:00.880
For the longest time, it seemed like this
was indeed the case.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:06.159
Leading airlines like Southwest were receiving
dozens of 737 maxes and they were being used

00:01:06.159 --> 00:01:07.160
almost immediately.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:14.700
Within a year of launch, 130 planes had been
delivered to 28 different airlines, and maxes

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:20.820
across the world had flown 42,000 flights,
transporting 6.5 million people.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:26.170
At this point, I think it was safe to say
that the 737 max was proving to be just another

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.170
routine upgrade for Boeing.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:32.330
But, all of this would change on October 29,
2018.

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:38.650
At 6:20 AM, Lion Air flight 610 took off from
Jakarta, Indonesia.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:43.400
And almost immediately, the pilots started
noticing some weird things.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:48.270
The plane was telling them that the aircraft
was in danger of stalling, and it appeared

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:51.159
that the flight controls were all screwed
up.

00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:56.080
The speed the altitude readings were way off
and it felt like the nose of the aircraft

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.460
was being pushed down.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.399
The pilots promptly contacted air traffic
control and requested permission to return

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:06.259
to Jakarta, but it was already too late.

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:11.360
Just 13 minutes after takeoff, air traffic
control would lose contact with Flight 610.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.530
They would go ahead and inform the National
Search and Rescue Agency who would deploy

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:20.780
three ships and a helicopter around the area
in which contact was lost, and what they found

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.209
was devastating.

00:02:22.209 --> 00:02:28.150
Flight 610 had crashed into the Java Sea near
an oil platform, killing all 189 people on

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:29.260
board.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:33.890
Workers on the nearby oil platform said the
plane was essentially uncontrollably nosediving

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:35.209
before crashing.

00:02:35.209 --> 00:02:40.989
While this was no doubt an international tragedy,
most people didn’t look into it much more

00:02:40.989 --> 00:02:42.830
than just that.

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:46.650
After all, Lion Air had a history of safety
concerns and poor management.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:51.970
In fact, they were even banned from flying
in European airspace up until 2016.

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:57.690
So, much of the world just wrote it off as
an isolated incident and issued a warning,

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:00.459
but the 737 max remained in service.

00:03:00.459 --> 00:03:04.610
Flash forward 5 months and it seemed like
things were going fine.

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:09.940
737 maxes had been routinely flying for nearly
2 years now making it more and more likely

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:13.420
that Flight 610 truly was just a one off tragedy.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:17.070
But, this would change on March 10, 2019.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:24.069
At 8:38 AM, Ethiopian Airlines flight 302
took off from Addis Ababa, Ethiopia.

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:28.489
Just one minute into the flight, the pilots
started reporting flight control issues and

00:03:28.489 --> 00:03:31.610
that the aircraft was pitching downward.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:37.690
Just 5 minutes later, the plane nosedived
into the ground at 700 miles per hour, once

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:40.410
again killing everyone on board.

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:42.880
This crash was taken much more seriously.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.989
It was extraordinarily unlikely that the same
issue took place twice within a matter of

00:03:47.989 --> 00:03:50.260
months just by chance.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:56.730
Within the next 72 hours, 737 maxes were grounded
around the world as it was clear who was at

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:57.730
fault.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:01.670
Since then, much of the companies’ leadership
has been ousted, Boeing has paid billions

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:06.930
of dollars in fines and settlements, and public
sentiment has very much turned against Boeing.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:12.230
But, likely the most haunting evidence that
has come out since then is a 117 page report

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:16.720
detailing internal communications at Boeing
regarding the 737 Max.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:20.109
One Boeing employee said quote “This is
a joke.

00:04:20.109 --> 00:04:21.739
This airplane is ridiculous.”

00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:27.720
And this was way back in 2016, years before
the either incident.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:32.240
Another employee wrote “I honestly don’t
trust many people at Boeing.”

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:37.810
Clearly, engineers at Boeing knew that the
737 Max wasn’t fit to fly, yet the aircraft

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:42.380
managed to pass all of its inspections with
flying colors, and hundreds of millions of

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:46.490
people continue to fly in their aircraft including
many world leaders.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:51.320
But, if even boeing engineers don’t trust
Boeing, why should we?

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:57.100
A HISTORY OF NEGLIGENCE:
If you look at the situation at face value,

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:02.229
you’ll probably think that the 737 max 8
was just a one off incident.

00:05:02.229 --> 00:05:07.400
After all, Boeing has been manufacuting planes
for over a hundred years and they rarely have

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:08.840
such issues right?

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:15.750
Well, this is true, it is rare that Boeing
has issues, but the key thing to note is why

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:18.319
they have issues.

00:05:18.319 --> 00:05:23.830
Boeing has produced tens of thousands of aircraft
over the decades, so it only natural that

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:27.510
they have a few hiccups here and there regardless
of how careful they are.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:33.740
If this was the fate of the 737 Max 8, it
would actually be somewhat understandable,

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:39.190
but the 737 Max 8 wasn’t caused by an honest
oversight or bad luck.

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:43.740
It was caused by deep rooted negligence and
ignorance that has been rampant within the

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:49.630
company for decades, and the 737 Max 8 was
by no means the first example of this.

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:54.760
One of the first examples of their negligence
dates back to the late 1980s with a cargo

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:55.979
door incident.

00:05:55.979 --> 00:06:01.380
The story starts off on March 10, 1987 with
a Pan Am flight from London to New York.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:05.870
The journey started off perfectly fine and
would go smoothly up until the plane reached

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:11.319
an altitude of 20,000 feet at which point,
the crew noticed that the cabin was depressurizing.

00:06:11.319 --> 00:06:16.840
So, the pilots would decrease the altitude
to 15,000 feet at which point the pressurization

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:18.789
problems disappeared.

00:06:18.789 --> 00:06:22.660
Thinking that the first occurrence was just
a fluke, the pilots would increase the altitude

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:27.440
to 20,000 feet once again, and like clockwork
the problems would reappear.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.990
Fortunately, the pilots didn’t try to push
the boundaries and just turned around and

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.389
returned to London.

00:06:33.389 --> 00:06:37.970
Once the plane was on the ground, the aircraft
was examined which revealed that all of the

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:42.590
cargo door locking arms had either been damaged
or completely sheared off.

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:47.060
Boeing would initially blame this incident
on the ground crew mishandling the cargo door,

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:51.290
but a few years later, it would become clear
who was truly at fault.

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:56.300
On February 24, 1989, United Airlines flight
811 would take off from Los Angeles heading

00:06:56.300 --> 00:06:57.319
towards Sydney.

00:06:57.319 --> 00:07:01.990
The flight had two intermittent stops on the
way though at Honolulu, Hawaii and Auckland,

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:02.990
New Zealand.

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:07.680
The first leg of the flight was completed
flawlessly with no concerns or issues.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:12.080
The second leg started off fine too up until
the flight crossed 20,000 feet at which point

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:14.740
everyone on board heard a load thump.

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:19.490
Before anyone could even react, the cargo
door swung open with such force that it ripped

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:23.520
open the entire aircraft at 22,000 feet in
the air.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:28.640
9 passengers would be catapulted out of the
aircraft and would all die.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:33.540
Everyone else on board assumed that an explosion
had gone off and that they were under attack.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:38.080
Miraculously, the pilots were able to turn
around the aircraft and return to Honolulu.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.490
And once the plane was on the ground, the
flight crew expeditiously unloaded everyone

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:47.199
within just 45 seconds, terrified that another
explosion would go off.

00:07:47.199 --> 00:07:51.930
But, after extensive research that lasted
years, it was determined that cause of the

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:57.560
incident was not an explosion but rather faulty
engineering in the cargo door that could have

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:01.090
been fixed if Boeing took the first incident
more seriously.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:05.599
This in itself is pretty troubling, but the
story gets even worse.

00:08:05.599 --> 00:08:11.160
26 years later in 2015, an FAA investigator
would complete a routine checkup on Boeing’s

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:15.940
factories and suppliers when he ran into a
mechanic working on a 777 cargo door.

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:19.740
When questioned about his work, the mechanic
would tell the inspector that “he does not

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:24.069
use the inspection tools required and enters
false inspection data on the work order.”

00:08:24.069 --> 00:08:29.080
Furthermore, “He admitted to falsely entering
the data for approximately 7-8 years.”

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:34.030
It’s not that the mechanic didn’t want
to perform thorough inspections, but rather

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:38.130
that the quotas from Boeing were so high that
he didn’t have the time or resources to

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:39.240
do so.

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:44.590
And if this mechanic alone had been doing
this for 7-8 years, imagine how many decades

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:47.850
this issue may have been ongoing at Boeing.

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:51.770
This may have been the reason for the United
Airlines disaster in 1989.

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:57.080
But, the fine for all of this was a measly
$12 million.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:03.890
PROFITS OVER LIVES:
The cargo door incident and the 737 max incident

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:07.260
are just two of many Boeing incidents in recent
history.

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:12.010
787 dreamliners have had issues with leaking
fuel, they’ve also had issues with batteries

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:18.600
catching on fire, and FAA has found inadequate
inspections techniques on multiple occasions.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:21.820
But, how did Boeing get so bad?

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:24.930
They obviously weren’t always bad.

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:29.339
At one point in history, they were the leaders
in the aviation industry pushing forward innovation

00:09:29.339 --> 00:09:32.130
and bringing out aircraft like the 747.

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:34.500
How did they go from that to this?

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:38.899
Well, usually, with these sorts of things,
there’s never a singular reason.

00:09:38.899 --> 00:09:43.649
But, one large catalyst that many aviation
experts point to is the McDonnell Douglas

00:09:43.649 --> 00:09:45.940
acquisition in 1997.

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:49.660
Before this acquisition, Boeing was largely
filled with engineers.

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:54.000
In fact, people often described Boeing as
an association of engineers as opposed to

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:55.649
an actual company.

00:09:55.649 --> 00:10:01.700
Obviously, Boeing themselves were slowly shifting
away from this by the late 1990s, but MD aggressively

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:02.800
accelerated this transition.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:06.690
You see, MD on the other hand was the exact
opposite of Boeing.

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:11.970
They had a reputation of cutting corners and
maximizing profits over everything else.

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:17.110
Considering this, I don’t know why Boeing
decided to acquire them, but they did and

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:19.410
almost immediately, you could see the effect.

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:23.590
If you’re a fan of the aviation industry,
you’d know that a substantial amount of

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:27.830
Boeing’s manufacturing and engineering takes
place in Seattle, Washington, so you would

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:33.880
assume that their headquarters were also in
Seattle, but this is not the case.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:39.370
After the MD acquisition, the HQ was moved
from Seattle to Chicago.

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:43.970
The official reasoning was that Boeing wanted
a more central location for their HQ.

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:49.740
But, in reality, it seems that MD was able
to convince Boeing to separate their engineers

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:51.339
and leadership.

00:10:51.339 --> 00:10:54.990
This would make it easier for leadership to
implement questionable cost cutting measures

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:59.710
while minimizing backlash from engineers,
and that’s exactly what they did.

00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:04.130
Over the next couple of years, Boeing would
aggressively outsource airplane construction

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:06.680
integrating more than 50 partners.

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:10.399
Pretty soon, they got it to a point where
the majority of parts weren’t even produced

00:11:10.399 --> 00:11:11.570
by Boeing factories.

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:16.430
These factories turned into assembly plants
where premade parts were just bolted together.

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:23.870
In fact, less than 40% of the 787 is actually
built by Boeing, and this is extremely dangerous

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:25.010
given their industry.

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:31.029
When Apple’s contractors screw up and their
iPhones end up bending, it’s not a big deal.

00:11:31.029 --> 00:11:35.700
But when Boeing’s contractors screw up and
the flaps don’t work or the cargo door doesn’t

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:38.389
close, it’s deadly literally.

00:11:38.389 --> 00:11:44.200
Nonetheless, Boeing would double down on this
path, and in 2005, they would hire their first

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:48.209
CEO without aircraft engineering experience:
Jim McNerney.

00:11:48.209 --> 00:11:53.200
To this day, insiders joke that McDonnel Douglas
bought Boeing with Boeing’s money.

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:58.639
I think you can guess why this is such a popular
joke.

00:11:58.639 --> 00:12:03.760
CHANGE?:
With such unsettling evidence coming out against

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:07.630
Boeing, you would think that they would make
sweeping changes.

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:13.270
But, this is not the first time that such
evidence has come out against Boeing.

00:12:13.270 --> 00:12:17.000
And if they didn’t change in the past, it’s
not a very compelling case that they’ll

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:18.000
change now.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:23.130
And the truth is, they have no reason to change
because they wont face any consequences.

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:28.300
Sure, they had to pay a hefty fine and sit
through a bunch of media backlash, but at

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:33.220
the end of the day, they still have a duopoly
with Airbus and no one is challenging that.

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:38.610
It’s not like people are going to stop going
on business trips or vacations or any of that.

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:43.330
And if we’re being honest, how many people
even know what aircraft they’re flying on.

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:47.190
They just book the cheapest flight or the
most convenient flight.

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:51.459
They might have a preference for the airline,
but they almost never have a preference for

00:12:51.459 --> 00:12:54.560
the aircraft itself unless they’re aviation
nerds.

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:58.360
So, it’s not like Boeing’s reputation
is really going hurt their top line over the

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:01.990
long term and that’s just the aviation side
of things.

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:06.060
If we look at the space and defense side of
Boeing, they have so many government contracts

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:09.330
worth tens of billions that it’s not even
funny.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:10.870
There is some hope though.

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:15.230
While it doesn’t look like the government
or the public will hold Boeing accountable,

00:13:15.230 --> 00:13:18.050
it looks like their own questionable practices
are.

00:13:18.050 --> 00:13:21.760
It turns out that outsourcing everything to
people who don’t know what they’re doing

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:23.940
doesn’t actually work over the long term.

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:26.240
I mean , who would’ve thought?

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:31.050
Excessive outsourcing has resulted in several
production flaws with the 787 which has severely

00:13:31.050 --> 00:13:33.529
limited production and deliveries.

00:13:33.529 --> 00:13:37.450
This has resulted in Boeing losing billions
of dollars every quarter.

00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:42.880
In fact, they haven’t had a single profitable
12 month period since the 737 fiasco.

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:47.130
And you can’t blame this on Covid either
given that Airbus is more profitable than

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:48.130
ever.

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:53.060
So, it looks like Boeing is finally getting
a taste of their own medicine, but that’s

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:54.060
just what I think.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:55.399
Do you trust Boeing?

00:13:55.399 --> 00:13:56.960
Comment that down below.

00:13:56.960 --> 00:14:00.320
Also, drop a like if you’re glad that Boeing
is finally feeling some pain.

